  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>There is no fixed number of months. HSE asks for regular review, and for a review whenever something changes. Here is what that means in practice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>HSE sets no fixed review interval.</li><li>Review regularly, and at once if the assessment may be invalid or the work changes significantly.</li><li>Also review after workers report problems, or after accidents or near misses.</li><li>Twelve months is a common house rule. It is your choice, not HSE's number.</li><li>Quickler records the review date. It does not send reminders.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The rule",
      "heading": "What HSE says",
      "html": "<p><a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/faq.htm\">HSE COSHH frequently asked questions</a>: there is no fixed interval. It depends on the type of risk, the work and how likely change is. Review at once if the original may no longer be valid or the work changes significantly. There is still an absolute requirement to review regularly. The law is <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2002/2677/contents\">the COSHH Regulations 2002</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Triggers",
      "heading": "What should prompt a review",
      "html": "<p>From <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/basics/assessment.htm\">HSE: how to carry out a COSHH risk assessment</a>: review if controls may no longer be effective, after changes to staff, a process, or the substances or equipment used, and after workers report problems, or after accidents or near misses. Also review the controls themselves to confirm they work.</p>"
